## THE HYPOTHESIS AND THE ALTERNATIVES (tested, none assumed; the drafter's reading is a prediction that your instruments may refute)
**H1 (the product owner-side symptom's likeliest cause, per the records):** the serverless SQL database auto-paused; the first signed-in reads after a pause take about 45-65 s to answer; the BFF cuts every non-POST call at its own **10 s** (`web/src/server/bff.ts:143` `UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS = 10_000`; `:193` `upstreamTimeoutMs`; `:351` answers 504 code `upstream-timeout`), the browser client asks a cut read **once** more (`web/src/api/readRetry.ts:14-42`, header: "asked ONCE more"), so the two tries cover about 20 s and the screen's `ErrorState` shows (`EngagementsList.tsx`: "The engagements could not be loaded", with `describeFailure` for a 504 at `web/src/api/client.ts:328`: "This is taking longer than usual ... reload the page in a minute"). By the time he refreshed, the database was awake. **Predicted signature:** a 504 `upstream-timeout` (twice, about 10 s apart) on `/api/v1/customers` (the call `useDirectory.ts:24` makes first; `:28-37` then reads up to 25 customer details), a SQL resume record in the ARM activity log within about a minute before or at 05:15Z, then clean 200s.
**The five alternatives the seat must also test** (each with the signature that would make it the cause and the signature that refutes it):
- **A1 App Service cold start / idle unload of the web or API app.** Predicted if true: a container start (`Pulling image` / `Starting container` / `Site started`) in the platform log at about 05:1xZ, an ARM restart/start record, or `alwaysOn` false. Refuted if: `alwaysOn` true on both apps (`infra/modules/appservice.bicep:79` API, `:183` web, MEASURED in source; READ the live value too), no container-start line and 0 restart/start/stop activity records in 05:00-05:30Z.
- **A2 a BFF/proxy timeout shorter than the resume (the CONVERTER, not a rival cause of the slowness).** Report it as its own finding: the numbers (10 s x 2 = about 20 s) against the measured resume durations (below), so Tuesday can see it is the reason a slow read became an error.
- **A3 a token/session first-request race.** Signature: a **401** (the BFF clears the UAT level cookie on 401/410, `bff.ts:458`) or a **503 `signin-refresh-unavailable`** (`bff.ts:414`), NOT a 504; the first signed-in read after the sign-in. Refuted if the 4xx metric is flat and the 5xx metric moves in the window (`Http4xx` vs `Http5xx`).
- **A4 the API itself failing fast on SQL (a 500 carrying error 40613 or a login timeout) instead of the BFF cutting.** Signature: the failure returns in under 10 s, so `readRetry` does NOT ask again (it retries only the BFF's own 504); the API's `EnableRetryOnFailure()` is on (`api/src/HpsmPoc.Modules.Assessment/AssessmentModule.cs:102`, default policy: **you read the default from the EF Core version the API references (the `.csproj`/`Directory.Packages.props` at `d0eff4ca`) and from nothing remembered**), the connection string carries `Connect Timeout=30` (`appservice.bicep:101`). Tell A4 from H1 by status class and duration (response-time metric, the audit table below).
- **A5 a client-side fetch with no retry.** Code-proven, not logged: which failures `withReadRetry` retries (only a BFF 504 `upstream-timeout`, once) and which it passes to the screen (502, 503, 5xx, 4xx, network failure); `useAsync`'s `reload` and the "Try again" button (the product owner refreshed the page instead).
**A cause is "measured" only when an instrument that can fail says so; "consistent with" is the strongest word a code read alone earns.**

